One common way to introduce vulnerabilities in Java web applications is through **Unsafe Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize data from an untisrusted source (like a cookie or a POST parameter), an attacker can supply a specially crafted object that executes arbitrary code during the deserialization process.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.URL;

public class VulnerableApp {
    // This method is vulnerable to Remote Code Execution (RCE) via unsafe deserialization
    public void processUserData(InputStream inputStream) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        // User-controlled data is read directly into an ObjectInputStream
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);

        // Deserializing untrusted data allows an attacker to instantiate malicious gadget chains
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
        System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.toString());
    }
}
***

import java.io.*;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SecureApp {
    // A safe approach involves avoiding Java native serialization for untrusted data
    // Instead, use a data-only format like JSON with a strictly defined schema
    public void processUserData(String jsonInput) {
        // We use a library like Jackson or Gson to parse data
        // These libraries are configured by default to only map data to specific fields,
        // preventing the instantiation of arbitrary classes/gadgets.
        try {
            // Assume UserDTO is a simple POJO with no logic in its setters/getters
            UserDTO user = new UserDTO(); 
            // Mapping JSON to a specific class prevents the "vulnerable" deserialization attack
            // Example using a pseudo-code approach for a JSON parser:
            // user = objectMapper.readValue(jsonInput, UserDTO.class);

            System.out.println("Safely processed user: " + user.getName());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid data format provided.");
        }
    }
}

// Simple Data Transfer Object (DTO)
class UserDTO {
    private String name;
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
}
